One-way binding and two-way binding are two common data binding techniques used in software engineering to synchronize data between the model and the view in an application.

One-way binding:
In one-way binding, data flows in a single direction, from the model to the view. When the model is updated, the view reflects the changes, but any changes made in the view do not affect the model. This approach is simpler and easier to understand, as it involves a straightforward flow of data.

Implementation in Swift:
One-way binding in Swift can be implemented using various techniques, such as using closures or delegates to update the view when the model changes. For example, you can define a closure in your view controller that updates the UI elements when the model's properties change.

Example:
```swift
class ViewModel {
    var text: String = "" {
        didSet {
            updateViewClosure?(text)
        }
    }
    var updateViewClosure: ((String) -> Void)?
}

class ViewController: UIViewController {
    var viewModel = ViewModel()

    @IBOutlet weak var label: UILabel!

    override func viewDidLoad() {
        super.viewDidLoad()

        viewModel.updateViewClosure = { [weak self] text in
            self?.label.text = text
        }
    }
}
```

Two-way binding:
In two-way binding, data flows in both directions, from the model to the view and from the view to the model. This means that changes in the model update the view, and changes in the view update the model. Two-way binding is useful in scenarios where the user interacts with the UI elements, and those interactions need to be reflected in the model.

Implementation in Swift:
Two-way binding in Swift can be implemented using various techniques, such as using property observers or reactive programming libraries like RxSwift or Combine. These libraries provide mechanisms to observe changes in both the model and the view and synchronize them.

Example using Combine:
```swift
